Since the previous example focused on a **continuous line (freehand drawing)**, let's look at a different approach: **Point-to-Point Shape Drawing**.

In this example, instead of drawing a continuous line, the user clicks to place "anchor points." Once they have placed points, the app connects them. This is how professional vector tools (like Adobe Illustrator or Figma) work. This approach is much better if you eventually want to extrude these shapes into 3D objects in ThreeJS.

### Example: Point-to-Point Vector Drawing

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const VectorDrawingTool = () => {
  const [points, setPoints] = useState([]);
  const [isClosed, setIsClosed] = useState(false);

  const handleCanvasClick = (event) => {
    // If the shape is already closed, we stop adding points
    if (isClosed) return;

    // Get coordinates relative to the SVG element
    const svg = event.currentTarget;
    const rect = svg.getBoundingClientRect();
    const x = event.clientX - rect.left;
    const y = event.clientY - rect.top;

    setPoints([...points, { x, y }]);
  };

  const closeShape = () => {
    if (points.length > 2) {
      setIsClosed(true);
    } else {
      alert("Please place at least 3 points to close a shape!");
    }
  };

  const resetCanvas = () => {
    setPoints([]);
    setIsClosed(false);
  };

