To achieve a layout where items are paired on each line (1 & 2 on line one, 3 & 4 on line two), you need to change the logic from a simple loop to one that handles "chunks" of two.

Here is the professional way to implement this using the abstract pattern we discussed.

### 1. The Abstract Base Class
This remains the same, providing a blueprint for any menu in your game.

```java
import java.util.List;

public abstract class MenuPrinter {
    public abstract void printMenu(List<String> menuItems);
}
```

### 2. The Concrete Implementation (The "Grid" Layout)
In this version, we use a `for` loop that increments by **2** instead of 1. This allows us to grab two items at once and put them on the same line.

```java
import java.util.List;

public class FightMenuPrinter extends MenuPrinter {
    @Override
    public void printMenu(List<String> menuItems) {
        // Loop through the list, skipping by 2 each time
        for (int i = 0; i < menuItems.size(); i += 2) {

            // Get the first item of the pair
            String item1 = menuItems.get(i);

            // Check if a second item exists in the list to avoid IndexOutOfBoundsException
            if (i + 1 < menuItems.size()) {
                String item2 = menuItems.get(i + 1);
                // Print both on one line
                System.out.println(String.format("[%s] [%s]", item1, item2));
            } else {
                // Only one item left, print it alone on the line
                System.out.println(String.format("[%s]", item1));
            }
        }
    }
}
```

